<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix A</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Nonconformity Scores</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In conformal prediction, a <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nonconformity score</em> <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies how atypical a candidate label <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is for an instance <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> given the model’s output distribution <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>. We evaluate three standard multiclass scoring functions:</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Naïve (Inverse Probability).</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The simplest approach uses the complement of the predicted probability:</p>
<table id="A1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{naive}}(x,y)=1-p(y\mid x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>naive</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{naive}}(x,y)=1-p(y\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This yields smaller scores for high-probability labels, producing larger prediction sets for low-confidence predictions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptive Prediction Sets (APS)</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Romano et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
Let <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1},\pi_{2},\ldots,\pi_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1},\pi_{2},\ldots,\pi_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the classes sorted in descending order of their probabilities <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\pi_{1}\mid x)\geq p(\pi_{2}\mid x)\geq\cdots\geq p(\pi_{K}\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\pi_{1}\mid x)\geq p(\pi_{2}\mid x)\geq\cdots\geq p(\pi_{K}\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>. For a given label <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(y)</annotation></semantics></math> be its rank in this sorted order. The APS score is the cumulative probability mass up to and including label <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{aps}}(x,y)=\sum_{j=1}^{r(y)}p(\pi_{j}\mid x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>aps</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{aps}}(x,y)=\sum_{j=1}^{r(y)}p(\pi_{j}\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Regularized Adaptive Prediction Sets (RAPS)</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angelopoulos et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
RAPS extends APS by adding a linear rank-based penalty:</p>
<table id="A1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{raps}}(x,y)=\sum_{j=1}^{r(y)}p(\pi_{j}\mid x)+\lambda\cdot r(y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>raps</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{raps}}(x,y)=\sum_{j=1}^{r(y)}p(\pi_{j}\mid x)+\lambda\cdot r(y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is the regularization parameter controlling the size-coverage trade-off. In this work, we set <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments were conducted on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU using Google Colab Pro. Training was performed for 6 epochs with a batch size of 64, a learning rate of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the Adam optimizer. The best checkpoint was selected based on development set performance measured by Quadratic Weighted Kappa (QWK).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>CP Coverage Plots</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To better understand the behavior of our conformal prediction variants, we provide supplementary plots analyzing performance, coverage calibration, and set size trends across different miscoverage rates <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. In Figure <a href="#A1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ A.3 CP Coverage Plots ‣ Appendix A Appendix A ‣ mucAI at BAREC Shared Task 2025: Towards Uncertainty Aware Arabic Readability Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we show the relationship between miscoverage rate <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and Quadratic Weighted Kappa (QWK) for three conformal prediction methods on the dev-tune set. APS and RAPS maintain stable QWK across all <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values, consistently outperforming the baseline. The naïve method degrades sharply beyond <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> &gt; 0.2, indicating poor robustness when allowing larger miscoverage.
In Figure <a href="#A1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ A.3 CP Coverage Plots ‣ Appendix A Appendix A ‣ mucAI at BAREC Shared Task 2025: Towards Uncertainty Aware Arabic Readability Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we plot the actual coverage against the target coverage for Naïve, APS, and RAPS methods. All methods achieve coverage above the target across the range, indicating slight conservativeness. This effect is most pronounced for APS, which consistently overshoots the target coverage. Such conservative calibration ensures statistical validity but may produce larger prediction sets than necessary, potentially impacting their interpretability. Finally, figure <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ A.3 CP Coverage Plots ‣ Appendix A Appendix A ‣ mucAI at BAREC Shared Task 2025: Towards Uncertainty Aware Arabic Readability Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the relationship between the miscoverage rate <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and the average prediction set size for the three nonconformity scoring methods. For <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, APS and RAPS yield larger sets than the naïve method, with APS producing the widest sets.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15485v1/qwk_vs_alpha.svg" id="A1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/295;" width="476" height="295"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Quadratic Weighted Kappa performance vs. miscoverage rate (<math id="A1.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) for three conformal prediction scoring methods on the dev-tune split. The dashed line represents baseline performance without conformal prediction.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15485v1/coverage_calibration.svg" id="A1.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/295;" width="476" height="295"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Coverage calibration quality showing actual vs. target coverage rates. The dashed line represents perfect calibration where actual coverage equals target coverage.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15485v1/setsize_vs_alpha.svg" id="A1.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/295;" width="476" height="295"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Average prediction set sizes across miscoverage rate (<math id="A1.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) for the three conformal prediction scoring methods.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Preprocessing &amp; Loss Ablations</h3>

<figure id="A1.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A1.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A1.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loss</span></th>
<th id="A1.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input</span></th>
<th id="A1.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QWK</span></th>
<th id="A1.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acc<sup id="A1.T3.2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">19</span></sup></span></th>
<th id="A1.T3.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">±1 Acc<sup id="A1.T3.2.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">19</span></sup></span></th>
<th id="A1.T3.2.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dist</span></th></tr>
